@isub $708E CALL BriefingMap

@bank $C079 DragonflyTitleBox 6

@isub $70B7 CALL DragonflyTitleBox

@bank $D27C DrawPointer 6

@isub $7202 CALL DrawPointer

@bank $C4C4 FillPolygon 3

@isub $7298 CALL FillPolygon

@bank $D08C DrawDebris 3

@isub $72A9 CALL DrawDebris

@bank $D1EC MoveAllDebris 3

@isub $72AC CALL MoveAllDebris

@bank $C9A7 DrawLine 3

@isub $72B9 CALL DrawLine

@bank $C000 StartTune 4

@isub $72CD CALL StartTune

@bank $C0D2 MusicTick 4

@isub $72D8 CALL MusicTick

@bank $C1A2 StopMusic 4

@isub $72EF CALL StopMusic

@bank $D58C UpdatePanelBars 6

@isub $732D CALL UpdatePanelBars

@bank $FE95 UnpackPicture 7

@isub $74B4 CALL UnpackPicture

@bank $CFCD SetClipWidth 3

@isub $74D7 CALL SetClipWidth

@bank $CBF4 ShowWaitPointer 6

@isub $758E CALL ShowWaitPointer

@isub $7591 CALL DrawPointer

@bank $CA23 CopyMissionTextB6 6

@isub $7594 CALL CopyMissionTextB6

@bank $D251 RestorePointerBackground 6

@isub $83C7 CALL RestorePointerBackground

@bank $D0CA SavePointerBackground 6

@isub $83CD CALL SavePointerBackground

@bank $D142 CopyViewUnderPointer 6

@isub $83D3 CALL CopyViewUnderPointer

@isub $83D6 CALL DrawPointer

@bank $D1BE DrawPointerInView 6

@isub $83D9 CALL DrawPointerInView

@isub $840D CALL RestorePointerBackground

@isub $8413 CALL SavePointerBackground

@isub $8416 CALL DrawPointer

@bank $DA09 QuitPrompt 6

@isub $8448 CALL QuitPrompt

@bank $CD63 Delay 6

@isub $84FF CALL Delay

@isub $8555 CALL Delay

@module system ; The 128K system area and the loader's leftovers at $5B00
@about The game image starts at $5B00. $5B00-$5BFF holds what the SpeedLock loader left there (the 128K ROM's own routines at $5B00 were overwritten by the bank 5 block, tape block 23); the game never reads or runs it. After a reset the ROM writes its SWAP routines here again, which is why the controls recording runs $5B00 (frames 330-405, after the reset).
$5B00 b LoadLeftover5B00 16 ; Leftover bytes of the load in the 128K ROM's system area
> No routine of the game reads or runs `$5B00-$5BFF` in any recording after the entry (the L1 of every play and menu build; controls runs the ROM's own SWAP here only after the reset at frame 331, `case.json` "overwritten"). Kept as a documented leftover.

@module mathtables ; The 3D engine's tables in bank 5: logarithms, squares, sines, screen rows, fill patterns, work lists
@about Read-only tables at $5C00-$66FF built into the bank 5 block, and the 3D work lists at $6680-$674F. The divide is done with logarithms ($5C00-$5EFF), the signed multiply with quarter squares ($5F00-$60FF), the rotations with three sine tables ($6100-$64FF). $6500 is the screen row table the view blits use. Witness: the L1 read ranges of the routines named in each entry (play, play2, missions, death).
$5C00 b LogFraction 32 ; log2 fractions: byte n = 256 * frac(log2 n), n = 1..255
> 256 bytes in octave runs: `$5C01`, `$5C02-$5C03`, `$5C04-$5C07`, ... `$5C80-$5CFF` (n = 128..255: `$00 $03 $06 ... $FF`). `$5C00` is 0 (log of 0 is not taken). With LogInteger, log2 n = `$5D00`[n] + `$5C00`[n] / 256 in 8.8.
> Readers: Divide `$A687` / DivideByLog `$A6D2` (`LD H,$5C` / `INC H`), bank 3 `$C959` (the polygon slopes). L1 play: `$A6D2` reads `$5C00-$5EFF`.

$5D00 b LogInteger 32 ; log2 integer parts: byte n = floor(log2 n); `$5D00` = `$F0`
> 256 bytes: 0 for n = 1, 1 for 2-3, 2 for 4-7, ... 7 for 128-255; byte 0 is `$F0` (a large negative log, so a zero numerator gives a zero quotient). Read beside LogFraction by `$A687`, `$A6D2`, bank 3 `$C959`.

$5E00 b ExpFraction 32 ; Antilogs: byte n = 256 * (2^(n / 256) - 1)
> 256 bytes `$00 $01 $01 $02 ... $FD $FF`: the 8-bit mantissa of 2^(n/256), with an implied leading 1 (`LD H,$01` after the lookup). Readers: DivideByLog `$A6D2` (`LD H,$5E`), bank 3 `$C959`.

$5F00 b SquareLow 16 ; Squares of the signed bytes, low bytes: `$5F00`[i] = low(i*i), i = -128..127 as a byte
> With SquareHigh the 16-bit square of the signed byte i: `$5F00`+i low, `$6000`+i high (i = `$81` gives 127 squared, i = `$80` 128 squared). Readers: the quarter-square multiplies MulSigned `$A7BF`, MulFrac `$A7F2`, MulWordByte `$A82C` (L1 play: `$5F00-$60FF`).

$6000 b SquareHigh 16 ; Squares of the signed bytes, high bytes: `$6000`[i] = high(i*i)
> Rises `$00`..`$3F` for i = 0..127, `$40` at i = `$80`, falls back for i = `$81`..`$FF` (the squares of -127..-1).

$6100 b Sine16Low 16 ; Sine, 16 bits, low bytes: sin(2 pi n / 256) * `$2000`
> 256 entries, low byte here and high byte at Sine16High `$6200` (`$6140`/`$6240` = `$2000`, `$61C0`/`$62C0` = `$E000`). Read by SinCos16 `$A8A5` (the 16-bit box BoxRect16 `$AAF5`).

$6200 b Sine16High 16 ; Sine, 16 bits, high bytes
> See Sine16Low `$6100`.

$6300 b Sine8 16 ; Sine, 8 bits: sin(2 pi n / 256) * 127, signed
> 256 bytes `$00 $03 $06 ... $7F ... $81 ...`; the cosine is the entry n + `$40`. Readers: SinCos8 `$A899` (the mission vector `$67E8`), the boxes `$AA63`, `$AB7B`, `$ABFF`, the polar points `$A119`, SinCosHalf `$A8B2`.

$6400 b Sine8Half 16 ; Sine, 8 bits, half a step on: sin(2 pi (n + 0.5) / 256) * 127
> 256 bytes `$02 $05 $08 ...`. Read only by SinCosHalf `$A8B2` when the angle word has its half-step bit (bit 7 of L) set; no recording reads it (known only from the code).

$6500 w ScreenRowTable 8 ; The screen address of each pixel row, rows 0..191
> 192 words `$4000`, `$4100`, ... `$57E0`. The view blits pop them with `SP` pointed into the table: ViewBlitFront `$8999` from `$6540` (row 32), ViewBlitWide `$8A41` from `$6580` (row 64), ViewBlitNarrow `$8A98` from `$6550` (row 40).

$6680 b PolyXClipped 8 ; Polygon work: the list clipped in X, two bytes a vertex (x, y)
> Written by the X clip of bank 3 (`$C218` and its states) for the span filler `$C4C4` / `$C542`, which also marks the top vertex (bit 7 of its y) and copies the list once more behind itself (`LDIR` at `$C514`) so the edge walkers can run round it. Up to 20 vertices.

$66A8 b PolyYClipped 3 ; Polygon work: the list clipped in Y, three bytes a vertex (x word, y byte)
> Written by the Y clip of bank 3 (`$C000`, `$C02F`, `$C0DE`, `$C17B`) from PolyVerts `$6700`, read by the X clip `$C218`.

$66CC b PolyClipVars 3 ; The near-plane clip's state: the first vertex (`$66CC`), the vertex count (`$66CD`), spare
> Set by FillFacesClipped `$9E20` / FillPolysClipped `$A07E` and read by ClipPolyNear `$A509`.

$66CF b PolyWorkSpare 6 ; Unused bytes before the vertex counter

$66D5 b PolyOutCount 1 ; The number of vertices ClipPolyNear `$A509` / AppendPolyVertex `$A677` put in PolyVerts

$66D6 b FillPatterns 2 ; Fill patterns: two bytes for each colour 0..12 (`$66D6` + 2c)
> The span filler `$C86A` (bank 3) takes (a, b) = (`$66D6`+2c, `$66D7`+2c) for the colour c in `$7579`: the row byte is b on one row and b XOR a on the next (the `XOR $00` at `$C931` is patched to a), so each colour is a 2-row dither: 0 `00/00` black, 1 `00/FF` row stripes, 2 `00/AA`, 3 `FF/FF` solid ink, 4 `FF/AA`, 5 `AA/AA`, 6 `AA/55` checker, 7 `FF/33`, 8 `55/11`, 9 `FF/55`, 10 `11/01`, 11 `33/11`, 12 `44/44`. The model scripts use colours 0-9. With `$757A` = 0 every colour is solid ink (`LD B,$FF` at `$C875`).

$66F0 b RightEdgeMasks 8 ; Keep masks right of a span end: `$7F $3F ... $00` for x AND 7 = 0..7
> Read by the span filler `$C86A` at the span's right end.

$66F8 b LeftEdgeMasks 8 ; Keep masks left of a span start: `$00 $80 $C0 ... $FE`
> Read by the span filler `$C86A` at the span's left end and for a one-byte span (`$C93C`).

$6700 b PolyVerts 4 ; Polygon work: the screen vertices of the face being filled, four bytes each (x word, y word)
> Filled by the face renderers (`$9D99`, `$9E20`, `$9ECD`, `$A07E`, ClipPolyNear `$A509`) from ProjectedPoints `$FC00`; the vertex count goes to `$68A9`, the colour to `$7579`, then `$7290` pages bank 3 and calls the filler `$C4C4`. Up to 8 vertices (`$6700-$671F`).

$6720 b FaceWorkSpare 8 ; Bytes between the polygon list and the face flags

$6728 b FaceFlags 8 ; The visibility flag of each face slot 0..39: `$FF` front-facing, `$00` back-facing
> Written by TestFaces `$9BAF` (opcode 5) at `$6728` + slot (the slot base from the opcode's first operand): `$FF` when the screen cross product of three vertices is negative (counter-clockwise on screen), or when one or two of them are behind the eye; `$00` when all three are behind. Read by the face renderers, the edge lists (opcodes 4, 17) and the branches (opcodes 6, 7, 8, 10).

@module gamevars ; The game's variables in bank 5 below the entry
$6798 b GameVars 9 ; Game variables: the completion flags, world values, the control device
$6799 MissionsDone ; nine flags, one per mission 1-9: all non-zero unlock Grand Finale (LoadMissionIfAllowed $759A)
$67A2 WorldVars
$67B6 ControlDevice ; the chosen device 0-4 (ReadControls)
$67B7 RepeatX ; frames to the next X step
$67B8 LastDirections ; the last direction bits
$67BA RepeatY
$67BB MousePos ; the mouse's X and Y (ReadMouse)
$67BD MouseDelta

@module apivectors ; The vectors the mission code calls: one JP per bank-2 service
@about The mission block (side 2, loaded at bank 0 $DBC5) is code as well as data; it reaches the resident routines through this table of 22 JP instructions at fixed addresses, so the resident code may move between releases without relinking the missions. Recording play: every vector runs (L1 counts in each entry); the callers are in $E1xx-$EBxx of the mission block.
$67BE c ApiVector00 ; The mission code's service vectors: 22 JP instructions, $67BE-$67FF
> Each vector is a `JP` to a resident routine in bank 2 or 5: `$67BE` `$BFD1`, `$67C1` `$B766`, `$67C4` `$BCDA`, `$67C7` `$BD41`, `$67CA` `$C957`, `$67CD` `$CA3B`, `$67D0` `$A779`, `$67D3` `$A7AE`, `$67D6` `$A799`, `$67D9` `$A82C`, `$67DC` `$B660`, `$67DF` `$B6D1`, `$67E2` `$B282`, `$67E5` `$B6BD`, `$67E8` `$A899`, `$67EB` `$B611`, `$67EE` `$7040`, `$67F1` `$CC3C`, `$67F4` `$AA63`, `$67F7` `$AB7B`, `$67FA` `$AAF5`, `$67FD` `$AA47`. The mission code calls them (play: `$67D9` 220 482 times, `$67D6` 179 580).
> Witness: L1 15082 (death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).
$67C1 ApiVector01
$67C4 ApiVector02
$67C7 ApiVector03
$67CA ApiVector04
$67CD ApiVector05
$67D0 ApiVector06
$67D3 ApiVector07
$67D6 ApiVector08
$67D9 ApiVector09
$67DC ApiVector10
$67DF ApiVector11
$67E2 ApiVector12
$67E5 ApiVector13
$67E8 ApiVector14
$67EB ApiVector15
$67EE ApiVector16
$67F1 ApiVector17
$67F4 ApiVector18
$67F7 ApiVector19
$67FA ApiVector20
$67FD ApiVector21

$6800 b PlayWork 16 ; Play work area $6800-$68A7 (play writes $6800-$682F)

$68A8 b PanelGauges 6 ; The panel's gauges: five 6-byte records from $68AB, then the four slots' records from $68C9
> DrawWeaponBars `$72F9` redraws a record whose first byte (the value) changed; FlickerPlayer `$6B1D` tests `$68BD` (record 3) below 16.
$68AB GaugeRecords
$68C9 SlotGauges
$68E2 GaugeEnd
$68EA MissionClock ; seconds, minutes, hours (ClockTick $7DFB); minutes and hours printed by PrintClock

@module boot ; The entry: the IM 2 set-up and the jump to the title menu
$68F4 c Entry ; The game's entry from the bootstrap: stack, IM 2 table, border, then the title menu in bank 6
> Under `DI`: `SP` = `$81FF`, the 257-byte vector table `$8200-$8300` filled with `$83` (`I` = `$82`, every vector `$8383`), `JP $8383` at `$8181`, `IM 2`, `EI`; the border to black (`BorderBlack` `$6CEB`), bank 6 paged, `JP $C409` (the title menu). The eight `NOP`s at `$691A` are room left for a patch.
> Witness: c1 frame 23974 (the bootstrap's `JP $68F4`, docs/03-build-and-load.md); no recording after the entry runs it (L1 0).

$6928 c ResetCollisionTables ; Reset the collision tables of bank 0 before a mission: clear bit 7 of $C774-$C78A, fill $C648-$C773 with $FF, point the camera at the player
> Called from bank 6 `$CA8B` (the catalogue screen): pages bank 0, clears bit 7 of `$C774-$C78A` (the slot list), sets the 300 bytes from `$C648` (CollisionTables) to `$FF`, calls CameraToPlayer `$C357` (bank 0: all three addresses are bank 0's, bank 6 holds code there), returns with bank 6 paged (`JP $7278`).
> Witness: L1 15 (menu, missions, search).

@module missionflow ; The mission's start, the play loops of the three views, the play keys
@about StartMission $694B sets the play state from the mission block's header ($DBD9-$DBDA), draws the panel through bank 7 ($74AA), and then runs PlayLoop $6A0C until the flag at $6A6D (the operand of LD A,0 at $6A6C) says the view changes. The three views each have their own loop: the 3D view (PlayLoop), the map view ($6AB9) and the status / Dragonfly views ($6BC7, $6C93). The loops call the same world, object and enemy steps; only the drawing differs.
@about The key codes come from the interrupt's keyboard scan (ScanKeyboard $8910) in KeyCode $8979 with the new-key flag in bit 1 of KeyFlags $8978.
$694B c StartMission ; Start the loaded mission: the play state from the header, the panel, the counters, then PlayLoop
> Reads the mission block's header (`$DBDA` -> `$BBF2`, `$DBD9` -> `$BBFA`), clears `$BC33`, sets the 3D view's blit (`$83E6` = `$8999`), draws the panel (`DrawBank7Picture` `$74AA` with DE = `$DD75`), resets the view parameters at `$9A6C`-`$9ABB`, calls the bank-6 set-up `$D521`, the weapon bars (`$7336`, `$73BA`), `$C632`, `$AE42`; clears the play flags (`$8459`, `$6A6D`, `$87E0`, `$D689`, `$D4B8`), prints `$D520` if `$7578` is set, then runs `$BA4A`, `$CEAB` and the play keys and falls into PlayLoop.
> Witness: L1 27 (death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).

$6A0C c PlayLoop ; One frame of the 3D view: draw, keys, world, objects, enemies, HUD; loop until the view changes
> Calls in order: `$DB71`, the 3D frame (`$9882`), the play keys (`PlayKeys` `$6B3B`), `$B9F6`, the clipping marks (`ClearClipRow` `$6A9D`), `$BAAC`, the world (`$AD45`, `$AEA0`), `$BBF1`, `$BD98`, bank 3 `$D08C` / `$D1EC` (`$72A3`), `$BA40`, `$C05E`, `$BF53`, bank 6 `$C7BC` / `$C4CD`, `$988D`, the message timer (`$7D5D`), the weapon bars (`$7327`), the object table (`$C026`), `$6B1D`, the patched hook `$6A9C` and `$7256`, until the view flag `$6A6D` is non-zero; then the next view's loop through `$6A99`.
> Witness: L1 410 (death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).
$6A57 PlayLoopTail ; the second half of the frame, also run alone after a view change
$6A6C ViewChangeFlag ; LD A,n: n ($6A6D) non-zero ends the loop
$6A91 NextView
$6A9C LoopHook ; a RET the menus patch ; clear the flag and return into the loop $6A99 names (default the title menu $C409)

$6A9D c ClearClipRow ; Fill the 32 bytes $90F1-$9110 with $FF
> The row of the back buffer `$90F1` (its line 75 + 16) set to `$FF` once a frame of every view loop, before the world is drawn. Witness: L1 3626 (death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).

$6AAB c FillPanelRows ; Fill 24 character rows of 6 bytes of the screen through ScreenRowFill
> A = 0, C = 2, D = 6, E = `$18`, then `JP $6D22`. Called from bank 6 `$C53F` / `$D521`. Witness: L1 41 (death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).

$6AB9 c MapLoop ; The map view's loop: the map picture from bank 7, then the world and objects without the 3D frame
> Draws the map frame (`DrawBank7Picture` with DE = `$F9CD`), sets the blit to `$8A98`, then per frame `$9882`, `$B9F6`, `ClearClipRow`, `$BAAC`, `$AD45`, `$AEA0`, `$BBF1`, `$BD98`, `$BA40`, `$BF53`, `$C7BC`, `$C4CD`, `$988D`, `$C026`, `$7256` until `$6A6D`; then the 3D blit again and `NextView`.
> Witness: L1 1 (play).

$6B1D c FlickerPlayer ; Toggle the low nibble of object 0's byte +7 from a random number while $68BD is below 16
> HL = `$75F7` (object 0, +7): if the byte is non-zero, XOR its low nibble with a random nibble (`$67D0` -> `$A779`). Runs while `$68BD` < 16 (the damage of the tank? known only from the code). Witness: L1 3601 (death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).

$6B32 c PanelSetup ; Page bank 6, call its panel set-up $D521, page bank 0 back
> Witness: L1 27 (death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).

$6B3B c PlayKeys ; The play keys: a new key code in $8979 jumps to its action
> Returns unless bit 1 of `$8978` is set and the code differs from the last (`$6B45`, the operand of `LD C,0`). E: `$843F` (sound off), J and K: nothing (`$6BB0`), M: the map (`$705A`), D: the Dragonfly (`$6BC2`), 1-4: weapons (bank 6 `$C53F`), H: `$D52E`, N: `$87FC`, R: `$BA45`, B: `$CEA7`, Z: `$CEE5`, C: remembered in `$6B45`.
> Witness: L1 3602 (death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).
$6B45 LastKey ; the operand of LD C,n: the last key code acted on

$6B95 c MapKeys ; The keys of the map view: B to $6BC7, C back to the 3D view, D to the Dragonfly
> Witness: L1 3 (play).

$6BB0 c NoAction ; A RET: the action of the keys with nothing to do
> Witness: L1 14403 (death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).
$6BB1 SelectPlay
$6BB4 SelectLoop ; HL = the loop to run next ($6A99), A remembered, then the view flag set

$6BC2 c SelectDragonfly ; HL = $6C93, the Dragonfly loop, then SelectLoop
> Witness: L1 1 (play).

$6BC7 c StatusLoop ; The status view's loop: the panel through bank 7 and the status texts, no 3D frame
> Pages bank 6 for `$DBB6`, draws `DrawBank7Picture` DE = `$D325`, prints the help texts (`$6C8B`), sets the blit to `$DBBD`, then per frame the mission clock (`PrintClock` `$7E3A`), the world and objects and bank 6's `$DB15`, `$D3C9`, `$E5BE`, `$C0F7`, the keys (`StatusKeys` `$6C60`) until `$6A6D`.
> Witness: L1 10 (play, play2).

$6C44 c DragonflyStart ; The first pass of the Dragonfly view: its one-time set-up $6C56, then the clock and keys loop
> Witness: L1 1 (play).

$6C56 c DragonflyOnce ; A NOP patched to RET after the first call of $70B6
> Witness: L1 1 (play).

$6C60 c StatusKeys ; The keys of the status view: C to the 3D view, B and M to $704E / $705E, then the help texts
> Witness: L1 530 (play, play2).

$6C8B c PrintStatusHelp ; Print the help text $6550 with attribute $1A through $B7AE
> Witness: L1 10 (play, play2).

$6C93 c DragonflyLoop ; The Dragonfly's loop: the panel from bank 7 at $C000, the Dragonfly's bars, then world and objects
> Witness: L1 1 (play).

@module text ; The screen text and attribute primitives: the print engine with control codes
@about PrintString $6FB3 interprets a string: a byte below $20 is a control code through the table $6F8F (position, colour, numbers, attribute boxes), $20-$81 a character (the 8x8 font of bank 6 at $F5E4, from $20), $82 and above a token (a string through the word table at $9860), $FF the end. The second font, 5 bytes a glyph from "0" (SmallFont $7C59), is printed by $6DE8 at any pixel column.
$6CE4 b PatchSpace 7 ; Seven zero bytes (NOP) before BorderBlack: room for a patch

$6CEB c BorderBlack ; Border 0 (the stored colour $6CF1 cleared)
> Witness: L1 66 (c1, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).
$6CEF BorderNext ; the border one colour on (a debug aid, known only from the code)

$6CFB c AttrBox ; Fill an attribute box from a 5-byte record: width, height, column, attribute, row
> HL -> B (width), C (height), E (column), A (attribute), L (row); falls into AttrBoxAt. Witness: L1 350 (c1, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$6CFD c AttrBoxAt ; AttrBox with B already set (HL -> height, column, attribute, row)
$6CFE AttrBoxAtC
> Witness: L1 319 (c1, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$6D05 c AttrBoxRC ; Fill C rows of B attribute bytes with A' from row L, column E ($5800 + 32L + E)
> Witness: L1 70 (c1, menu, missions, play, play2).

$6D22 c ScreenRowFill ; Fill E pixel lines of D bytes with A from the line table $6500, column C
> Uses the line address table at `$6500` (2 bytes a pixel line). Witness: L1 200 (c1, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$6D42 c PointerZones ; When the pointer is clicked, call the zone of the current screen that holds the pointer
> `LD A,n`: n (`$6D43`) is set by the fire test of the interrupt (`$7DC2`). The zone table (HL, the operand at `$6D47`, set per screen by `SetScreen` `$7060`) has 6-byte records x1, x2, y1, y2 and a routine; the first record with x1 <= X < x2 and y1 <= Y < y2 (X `$9715`, Y `$9716`) is called. A 0 byte ends the table. Witness: L1 30082532 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).
$6D43 ClickFlag ; the operand: non-zero = a click to act on
$6D47 ZoneTable ; the operand: the current screen's zone table

$6D7F c CopyGraphic ; Copy a C x B graphic from HL' to the screen at pixel line A, column BC
> The line address table `$6500` again. Witness: L1 416 (c1, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

