@module raster ; The 3D view's raster: the back buffer, the blits to the screen, the clear, the projection, the clips, the arithmetic
@about The 3D view is drawn into a 32 x 96 byte back buffer at $8AF1-$96F0 (256 x 96 pixels, no attributes), cleared with PUSH once a pass ($96F1) and copied to the screen by one of three LDI blits that the interrupt runs when the pass is done ($83BA). The projection divides with logarithms (DivideByLog $A6D2 and the tables at $5C00); lines and polygons are clipped against the near plane in 3D ($A42B, $A509) and against the window in 2D ($A184 here, $C000/$C218 in bank 3). The filler and the line drawer live in bank 3 (frag-bank3.ann).
$898B c ViewBlitLow ; Another entry of ViewBlitFront: the back buffer from its row 8 ($8BF1) to the screen from row 40, 71 rows full width, then the cockpit rows
> Saves `SP` at `$8A3E`, HL = `$8BF1`, the row count `$47`, `SP` = ScreenRowTable `$6550`, then `JR` into ViewBlitFront's loop at `$89A5`. No routine stores `$898B` in the blit pointer `$83E6` and no recording runs it: known only from the code.

$8999 c ViewBlitFront ; Copy the back buffer to the screen rows 32-126: the front view with the cockpit cut-outs
> With `SP` on ScreenRowTable `$6540` (row 32): 79 rows of 32 bytes (`LDI` x 32), then 8 rows of 11 + 11 bytes (the middle 10 bytes skipped, the cockpit), then 8 rows of 2 + 2 bytes (the outer columns only). `SP` is saved at `$8A3E`. Runs under the interrupt (the routine pointer `$83E6`).
> Input: none. Output: the screen bitmap `$4080-$4CCA` (L1). Cost: 97 permille of play (the heaviest single routine of the view).
> Witness: L1 3600 (c1, death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).

$8A41 c ViewBlitWide ; Copy the 86 top rows of the back buffer to the screen rows 64-149, full width
> `SP` on ScreenRowTable `$6580` (row 64), 86 rows of 32 bytes. Used by the menus and the console views (11 recordings).
> Witness: L1 458 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$8A98 c ViewBlitNarrow ; Copy 95 rows of 22 bytes (back-buffer columns 5-26) to the screen rows 40-134 at column 6
> `SP` on ScreenRowTable `$6550` (row 40); each row `INC E` x 6, 22 `LDI`, skip 10. Only the play recording runs it (the binoculars or the rear view).
> Witness: L1 24 (play).

$8AEC c ClearBlitRequest ; Clear the blit request `$83BA`: the interrupt's last job after a view blit
> Witness: L1 6657 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$8AF1 b BackBuffer 32 ; The 3D view's back buffer: 96 rows of 32 bytes (256 x 96 pixels, row 0 at the top)
> Cleared by ClearBackBuffer `$96F1`, drawn by the span filler (bank 3 `$C86A`), the line drawer (bank 3 `$C9A7`) and PlotBackBuffer `$AA27`, read by the three blits. Row y starts at `$8AF1` + 32y. Its last 113 bytes (`$9680-$96F0`) are what game.ann calls InputLeftover: they are back-buffer rows 92-95, zeroed every pass.

$96F1 c ClearBackBuffer ; Zero the back buffer `$8AF1-$96F0` with 1536 PUSH HL
> Under `DI`: `SP` saved at `$9711`, `SP` = `$96F1`, HL = 0, 96 x 16 `PUSH HL`, `SP` back, `EI`. An interrupt in this time is lost (WALKTHROUGH section 1). Once per 3D pass: its count is the number of passes (705 in play).
> Witness: L1 6643 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

@module input ; The controls: keyboard, cursor, Sinclair, Kempston joystick and mouse
@about ReadControls $971A runs the reader of the device chosen in the options ($67B6, 0-4) through the table $9723. Every reader but the mouse ends in DecodeDirections $9758: the five bits right, left, down, up, fire, with acceleration (the step grows by one up to 8 while a direction is held, $9719 frames per step).
$9715 b ClickState 5 ; The click position (X, Y), the fire timer (on, count), the frames per step
$9715 ClickX
$9716 ClickY
$9717 FireHeld
$9718 FireCount
$9719 StepFrames

$971A c ReadControls ; Read the chosen device: the reader of $67B6 in the table $9723
> Witness: L1 69895 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$9723 w DeviceReaders 2 ; Five readers: Sinclair, cursor, Kempston mouse, Kempston joystick, keyboard

$972D c ReadSinclair ; Sinclair (Interface 2): port $EFFE
> Witness: known only from the code (entered by `JP (HL)`: menu runs it, L1 0 entries).

$9734 c ReadCursor ; Cursor keys: ports $F7FE, $EFFE, $7FFE
> Witness: known only from the code (entered by `JP (HL)`).

$9757 c InvertDirections ; CPL then DecodeDirections
> Witness: L1 1225 (menu).

$9758 c DecodeDirections ; Bits right, left, down, up, fire: the X and Y steps with acceleration ($985E, $985F), carry = fire
> Witness: L1 7771 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$9813 c ReadMouse ; Kempston mouse: X from $FBDF, Y from $FFDF, the deltas
> Reads X (`AND $7F`, doubled) and Y (inverted), stores them at `$67BB`, then the deltas over `$67BC-$67BD`: the delta word overwrites the new Y. It returns straight to the caller with A = the X delta and the carry of that subtraction: it never reaches `DecodeDirections`, so `$985E` / `$985F` keep their last values and `PointerFire` takes the carry for the fire button. Recording controls: 30 runs (frames 295-330) before the machine resets (see NOTES.md, open point 4). Witness: L1 0 (controls; entered by `JP (HL)`).

$9833 c ReadKempston ; Kempston joystick: port $1F, the bits reordered for DecodeDirections
> Witness: known only from the code (entered by `JP (HL)`; controls runs it).

$9844 c ReadKeys ; The keyboard: five keys from the table $9862 (port, mask)
> Witness: known only from the code (entered by `JP (HL)`; every recording runs it).

$985C b InputState 6 ; The mouse's last position, the X and Y steps, the token table base
$985E StepX
$985F StepY

$9862 b KeyTable 2 ; The five keys of ReadKeys: half-row high byte and bit mask

$9874 c CallTableEntry ; Jump to the word A of the table at HL
> Witness: L1 69895 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$987C c TableEntry ; HL = HL + 2A
> Witness: L1 69895 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

@module render ; The 3D display-list interpreter: the frame start, the opcodes, the model scripts' renderers, the box generators
@about A model is a script of byte opcodes read through IX by RunScript $990F until $FF; the opcode indexes the 23-word table $98E1. The vertices live in three rows of words at $FD00 (x), $FE00 (y), $FF00 (z), 64 vertices, in the model's own axes already turned by the object's heading (the box generators); ProjectScript $9A43 adds the object's position (the operands at $9A6C, $9A8E, $9AB3, set by DrawObject $B827) and writes the screen points to $FC00 (4 bytes a vertex, x = $8000 behind the eye). The opcodes test faces by their screen winding ($9BAF), fill the front faces through the bank 3 filler, draw lines through the bank 3 line drawer, and order the parts of a model by a face's visibility ($9D5A). Models: bank 3 $CA8B-$CFCC, bank 2 $8739-$87D5, bank 5 $7E8F-$7FAF, bank 0 $D5D0, $D934, $FE80, bank 6 $E512-$E560, and each mission block's library (mission 1: 31 models at $EC57-$FB3E).
$9882 c StartViewPass ; Start a 3D pass: wait until the last blit is done (`$83BA` = 0), clear the back buffer
> Spins on `$83BA` (set to `$FF` by RequestViewBlit `$988D`, cleared by the interrupt after the blit, ClearBlitRequest `$8AEC`), then calls ClearBackBuffer `$96F1`. Callers: the play loops `$694B`, `$6A0C`, `$6AB9` and the bank 6 menu views `$C409`, `$C4AD`, `$CA8B`, `$DA09`, `$DB1C`. Its own time (18 permille) is the wait for the interrupt.
> Witness: L1 6643 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$988D c RequestViewBlit ; Ask the interrupt to blit the finished back buffer: `$83BA` = `$FF`
> Witness: L1 6658 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$9893 c ClearBackBufferOld ; Unused: clear 1792 bytes below `$98EF` and fill 1792 bytes below with `$AA`/`$55` rows, both by PUSH
> Sets `SP` = `$98F1` and calls two PUSH loops that take their return address off the stack first. As placed it would overwrite OpcodeTable `$98E1` and the code below it; no caller, a leftover of an earlier layout.
> Witness: known only from the code (no recording runs it).

$98E1 w OpcodeTable 1 ; The 23 script opcodes' handlers, indexed by the opcode byte
> 0 `$992D` native call, 1 `$9938` vertex program, 2 `$9A43` project, 3 `$9AD8` lines, 4 `$9B38` lines by faces, 5 `$9BAF` face test, 6 `$9CB1` jump if face visible, 7 `$9CE8` the same, 8 `$9D28` branch on face, 9 `$9D1E` return, 10 `$9D5A` order by face, 11 `$9D0D` call, 12 `$9D8F` fill faces, 13 `$9E1E` fill faces with the near clip, 14 `$9ECD` fill polygons, 15 `$9F37` fill back faces, 16 `$9F43` lines with the near clip, 17 `$9FC3` lines by faces with the near clip, 18 `$A049` translate, 19 `$A07E` fill polygons with the near clip, 20 `$A114` fill back faces with the near clip, 21 `$A119` polar points, 22 `$B7E5` draw the carried object. Opcodes 4, 6, 7, 8, 17 are in no model of the recordings.

$990F c RunScript ; Run the model script at IX until the byte `$FF`
> Reads the opcode at IX, IX+1, patches the `CALL` at `$9927` with OpcodeTable[opcode] and calls it; every handler returns to `$992A` (`JP $990F`), with IX past its operands. Input: IX = the script; bank of the script paged (bank 3 via `$72BE`, bank 0 for the mission models). Callers: DrawObject `$B827` (the objects), `$B190` (the ground and the sky, scripts at bank 0 `$FF80`, `$FF8C`, `$FF98`), `$72BE` (bank 6 views with bank 3 models), the mission code `$E3F3`, `$E561`, and ScriptTranslate `$A049`, which jumps back here.
> Witness: L1 131277 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$992A c ScriptNext ; The handlers' return: go on with the next opcode
> Witness: L1 104358 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$992D c OpNative ; Opcode 0: jump to the native routine at the word operand
> IX on past the word, then `JP (HL)`: the routine reads its own operands through IX and returns to ScriptNext. The models call the box generators by the mission vectors `$67F4` (BoxSquare), `$67F7` (BoxRect), `$67FA` (BoxRect16), `$67FD` (BoxSquareTurret) and `$ABFF` (BoxTilted) with 3 operand bytes; other targets take none (`$F6B9` in mission 1 clears the tilt `$68E6`; `$D4A4`, `$D5C9`, `$E2C0`, `$EC2E`, `$EE06`, `$EE22`, `$FE8F` in bank 0).
> Witness: L1 15260 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$9938 c OpVertexProgram ; Opcode 1: build vertices from others with a byte program: load, store, midpoint, add, subtract, negate
> Under `DI` with `SP` used as a pointer into the vertex rows. The current point P = (DE, DE', BC') = (x, y, z). Bytes: `$00-$3F` n: P = V[n]; `$40-$7F`: V[n AND `$3F`] = P; `$80-$BF`: P = (P + V[n AND `$3F`]) / 2; `$C0` m: P = P + V[m]; `$E0` m: P = P - V[m]; `$F0`: P = -P; `$F8`: P.x = -P.x, P.z = -P.z (the half-turn about the vertical); any other byte (`$FF`) ends it, `SP` back, `EI`. With the box corners `V34-V41` from a box generator, midpoints and sums give every other vertex of the model in its turned axes.
> Witness: L1 10819 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$9A43 c OpProject ; Opcode 2 (n, 2k): project n vertices from V[k] to screen points in ProjectedPoints `$FC00`
> For each vertex: depth = z + the operand at `$9A6C` (the object's z, `$0190` by default); if depth < 96 the point is marked behind the eye (`$FC00`+4i = `$8000`). Otherwise sx = 128 + 256 (x + `$9A8E`) / depth (shifted right by the count at `$9A93` first, the zoom), sy = 64 - 256 (y + `$9AB3`) / depth, through Divide `$A687` and DivideByLog `$A6D2` (one logarithm of the depth for both). Output: `$FC00`+4i = sx word, `$FC02`+4i = sy word.
> Witness: L1 22966 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$9AD8 c OpLines ; Opcode 3: a polyline: `$80`+v moves to point v, v draws a line to it, `$C0` ends
> A line is drawn only when both ends are in front of the eye; ClipLine2D `$A184` clips it to the window and `$72B1` draws it with bank 3 `$C9A7`.
> Witness: L1 1542 (c1, death, m2erbe, menu, missions, play, play2).

$9B38 c OpLinesByFaces ; Opcode 4: edges drawn when either of two faces is visible: (face a, face b, v) or `$80`+v, `$C0` ends
> The wire edge between faces a and b (flags `$6728`+a, +b) is drawn to point v when one of them faces the eye, else the pen moves to v. In no model of the recordings (one use in mission 1 at `$F99E`, its model not drawn in them).
> Witness: known only from the code (no recording runs it).

$9BAF c OpTestFaces ; Opcode 5 (slot, n, n x (a, b, c)): set the visibility flags of n faces from their screen winding
> Slot base to `$9C6B` (+`$28`), then TestFace `$9BC3` for each triple of point offsets (4 x vertex).
> Witness: L1 9372 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$9BC3 c TestFace ; One face: the sign of the cross product (b - a) x (c - a) of three screen points into FaceFlags
> Counts the points behind the eye in `$9BDA`: 3 gives flag 0 (`$9CAA`), 1-2 give `$FF` (`$9CA3`, drawn and clipped later); else the two edge vectors are scaled to bytes (ScaleToBytes `$9C84`) and multiplied with MulSigned `$A7BF`: flag = `$FF` when ex1 * ey2 < ey1 * ex2.
> Witness: L1 45290 (c1, death, missions, play, play2).

$9C6A c StoreFaceFlag ; Store the face flag B at `$6728` + slot, next slot, loop over the triples
> Witness: L1 45290 (c1, death, missions, play, play2).

$9C84 c ScaleToBytes ; Halve the two words HL, DE together until both fit in a signed byte (C = the sign bias)
> The loop `$9C7C` shifts both right. Unreferenced code at `$9C9A` (another flag store) follows.
> Witness: L1 107436 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$9CA3 c FacePartlyBehind ; A face with one or two points behind the eye: flag `$FF`
> Witness: L1 11 (c1, death).

$9CAA c FaceAllBehind ; A face with all three points behind the eye: flag 0
> Witness: L1 1123 (c1, death, missions, play, play2).

$9CB1 c OpJumpIfVisible ; Opcode 6 (face, address): go on at the address when the face is visible, else skip 3 bytes
> Unreferenced code at `$9CD7` (the same with a return address pushed) follows.
> Witness: known only from the code (no recording runs it).

$9CE8 c OpJumpIfVisible2 ; Opcode 7 (face, address): the same as opcode 6
> Witness: known only from the code (no recording runs it).

$9D0D c OpCall ; Opcode 11 (address): run the script at the address as a subroutine (IX pushed, opcode 9 returns)
> Witness: L1 878 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$9D1E c OpReturn ; Opcode 9: return from a script subroutine: IX popped from the stack
> Used to end the parts of OpCall `$9D0D` and OpOrderByFace `$9D5A`.
> Witness: L1 12941 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$9D28 c OpBranchOnFace ; Opcode 8 (face, visible, hidden): go on at one of two addresses, the return pushed (IX + 5)
> Witness: known only from the code (no recording runs it).

$9D5A c OpOrderByFace ; Opcode 10 (face, part A, part B): draw the inline part and the two parts in an order set by the face
> Face visible: the inline part (IX + 5), then A, then B; hidden: A, then the inline part, then B. Each part ends with opcode 9; the two other entries are pushed as returns. This is how a model sorts its own parts (the turret before or behind the hull) without a depth sort.
> Witness: L1 6021 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$9D8F c OpFillFaces ; Opcode 12 (n, n x face): fill the visible faces; face = (slot, length, k, colour, k point offsets)
> Patches `$9DB2` to `JP Z` (skip hidden faces) and `$9DAD` to fall through, then shares FillFaceList `$9D99`. A face with a point behind the eye is skipped (`$9E0B`).
> Witness: L1 11209 (c1, controls, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$9D99 c FillFaceList ; Copy each chosen face's screen points to PolyVerts `$6700` and fill it with bank 3 `$C4C4` (via `$7290`)
> The colour to `$7579`, the count to `$68A9`, the face count in `$68AA`. A face whose flag fails the test is skipped by its length byte.
> Witness: L1 5753 (missions, play2).

$9E1E c OpFillFacesClipped ; Opcode 13: fill the visible faces, clipping those that cross the near plane
> Witness: L1 2455 (c1, death, missions, play, play2).

$9E20 c FillFaceListClipped ; As FillFaceList, but a point behind the eye calls ClipPolyNear `$A509` to cut the face at the near plane
> A = the opcode patched at `$9E40` (`JP Z` for opcode 13, `JP NZ` for 20). The first point offset is kept in `$66CC` for the closing edge.
> Witness: L1 5778 (c1, death, missions, play, play2).

$9ECD c OpFillPolys ; Opcode 14 (n, n x (k, colour, k point offsets)): fill polygons with no face test
> Witness: L1 5571 (c1, death, m2erbe, menu, missions, play, play2).

$9F37 c OpFillBackFaces ; Opcode 15: fill the faces whose flag is 0 (`JP NZ` at `$9DB2`): the insides seen through an opening
> Witness: L1 29 (missions, play2).

$9F43 c OpLinesClipped ; Opcode 16: a polyline like opcode 3, a segment with an end behind the eye cut at the near plane (`$A422`, `$A42B`)
> Witness: L1 785 (death, missions).

$9FC3 c OpLinesByFacesClipped ; Opcode 17: opcode 4 with the near clip
> Its near-clip calls `$A039`, `$A041` are unreferenced. In no model of the recordings.
